bash.exe.stackdump
*.stackdump

# Claude worktrees state
.claude/worktrees/

# gitleaks scan output (config is committed; reports are local-only)
gitleaks-report.json

# Example files
examples/

# Lakeside import cleaned data (real customer PII — never commit)

# ─── Customer / client PII — NEVER commit real customer data ─────────────────
# Onboarding & migration work (vendor migrations) produces
# raw vendor exports and ETL "staged" files full of names / addresses / phones /
# emails. Ignore by file TYPE so PII is caught no matter which folder it lands in.
# Source code never uses these extensions, so this hides nothing legitimate:
#   • spreadsheets (.xlsx/.xls/.csv) are always data dumps, never code
#   • *_staged.json = the load-ready ETL output (full PII payload)
# If you ever truly need to commit a real fixture, whitelist that ONE path below
# with a trailing "!" rule, e.g.:  !backend/src/__fixtures__/sample.csv
*.xlsx
*.xls
*.csv
*_staged.json
# The one whitelisted CSV: the catalog import template (header + one made-up
# example row, no customer data). Pinned by catalog-import-template tests.
!docs/inventory/catalog-import-template.csv
# ETL/import working dirs (raw exports + any transformed PII output)
md_files/plans/onboarding/**/*-etl/*.json
**/*Reports/
# ─── Agent + tooling scratch output (generated, never source) ────────────────
# These accumulate in the primary checkout and masquerade as uncommitted work.
# Measured 2026-08-05: ~1,050 untracked files, 41 MB, none of it source.
.playwright-mcp/
.vercel/
**/.impeccable/
.org-email-toggle-preview
# Vendored duplicate skill trees. The canonical copy Claude Code loads from is
# .claude/skills/ - these two are byte-identical mirrors of it.
.agents/
.github/skills/
# Screenshots dropped at the repo root during UI work.
/*.png
ai-work/env/

# ─── QA session artefacts — LIVE CREDENTIALS, never commit ───────────────────
# `scripts/qa-session.mjs --session` mints a REAL Supabase session for a real
# user and writes it to disk: the file holds both an access token and a refresh
# token, so anyone who gets it can act as that user until the refresh token is
# revoked. Gitleaks does not flag it (opaque JWTs inside JSON look like data, not
# a recognised key format), so `.gitignore` is the only thing standing between a
# stray `git add -A` and working credentials in the repo history.
# `.qa/` is where the script writes today; the root glob catches files left over
# from before it moved, and any future target beyond staging/prod.
.qa/
.qa-session-*.json

# ─── Agent-run session artefacts: LIVE CREDENTIALS, never commit ─────────────
# The Bug Fixes Department mints a REAL session per run and writes it to
# md_files/plans/agents/runs/issue-<n>/session/<env>.json. Same hazard as the
# block above: the file holds both an access token and a refresh token, so
# anyone who gets it can act as that user until the refresh token is revoked,
# which stays true long after the access token expires. Gitleaks does not flag
# it either, for the same reason.
# Until 2026-09-20 this path was held back only by .git/info/exclude, which is
# local to one machine and never travels with a clone, so the protection did not
# exist for anybody else. The whole runs/ tree is ignored here, matching what
# that local exclude already did, so nothing about which files get committed
# changes. The second rule is belt and braces if run directories are ever
# reorganised.
md_files/plans/agents/runs/
md_files/plans/agents/**/session/

# Orphan-sweep delete manifests (scripts/attachment-orphan-sweep.ts). A record of destroyed
# storage keys: useful locally, never a repo artifact.
backend/tmp/
